Create Group 07 — Data Display for the DevKit.

This group belongs to:

Layer 01 — Foundations & Interface Primitives.

Existing groups:

Group 01 — Interaction Controls
Group 02 — Form Controls
Group 03 — Selection Controls
Group 04 — Navigation Controls
Group 05 — Feedback & Status
Group 06 — Overlays & Surfaces

Use the DevKit Core Design Constitution as the immutable global foundation.

Do not redefine global design tokens.

Inherit all existing:
- colors
- semantic colors
- typography
- spacing
- sizing
- control heights
- radius
- borders
- elevation
- shadows
- iconography
- motion
- breakpoints
- responsive behavior
- accessibility
- focus treatment
- touch targets
- density
- Light theme
- Dark theme
- High Contrast
- RTL
- localization
- reduced motion
- reduced transparency

Create exactly these 10 components:

061 Card
062 List
063 List Item
064 Data Row
065 Avatar
066 Avatar Group
067 Key-Value Pair
068 Divider
069 Timeline
070 Content Block

These are content-display primitives.

They must be:
- reusable
- composable
- responsive
- accessible
- framework-independent at the design level
- suitable for both simple and complex applications

Do not create:
- dashboards
- complete profile pages
- complete product cards
- complete feeds
- complete tables
- complete activity pages
- complete settings pages
- complete detail pages

Those belong to higher-level layers.

The components must work equally well for:
SaaS
business applications
consumer applications
admin systems
AI products
data applications
documentation
commerce
productivity software

Do not make the components overly application-specific.

Prioritize information hierarchy.

Users should be able to quickly distinguish:
primary content
secondary content
metadata
status
supporting information
actions

Do not use excessive containers.

Not every piece of content needs a card.

Avoid unnecessary:
- borders
- shadows
- background surfaces
- decorative gradients
- excessive rounding

The design language should feel:
quiet,
precise,
spacious,
hierarchical,
premium,
and highly readable.

Content must remain the visual priority.

Do not let decoration compete with content.

All components must support:

Mobile
Tablet
Desktop
Large Desktop
Ultra Wide

All relevant components must support:

Light
Dark
High Contrast

Support:

Mouse
Keyboard
Touch
Stylus
Screen readers

Support:

LTR
RTL
Localization
Dynamic text scaling
Reduced motion
Reduced transparency

Long content must be handled gracefully.

Do not rely on fixed heights when content can legitimately grow.

Do not truncate important information without a discoverable way to access the full content.

Images must have appropriate aspect-ratio behavior.

Do not distort images to fit arbitrary containers.

Avatar fallback behavior must be deterministic.

Missing content must have appropriate fallback behavior.

Content hierarchy must remain understandable without color.

Interactive data-display components must use existing interaction primitives rather than inventing custom buttons.

Do not create nested interactive elements accidentally.

If a Card is clickable:
make the interaction model explicit.

If only one action inside a Card is interactive:
do not make the entire Card appear clickable.

Support:
hover
focus
pressed
selected
disabled
where the component is interactive.

Do not add hover effects to purely informational components.

Responsive behavior must be intentional.

Do not simply shrink desktop layouts.

For narrow screens:
allow content to stack
allow secondary metadata to move
allow rows to transform
allow horizontal scrolling only where genuinely appropriate

Never allow important content to become unreadably compressed.

Typography must preserve hierarchy across breakpoints.

Use the global typography scale.

Do not invent component-specific font sizes unless explicitly required by the design constitution.

Spacing must use existing spacing tokens.

Do not introduce arbitrary one-off spacing values.

All components must be compatible with localization.

Expect:
long labels
short labels
different scripts
RTL
large text
browser zoom

Do not design around English-only text.

Test:
empty content
long content
missing images
long names
long descriptions
large numbers
small numbers
localized text
RTL
large text
high zoom
keyboard
screen readers
touch
dark mode
light mode
high contrast
mobile
desktop
ultra-wide screens

The output must provide a coherent data-display family rather than ten visually unrelated components.
